ResearchPlanAbstract:
Ipomopsis aggregata (scarlet gilia) and I. tenuituba, both flowering herbs, hybridize at several localities over their range. The hybrid zone is comprised of a series of populations along which the morphological characters that distinguish the two species form a continuous cline. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;

Ipomopsis aggregata populations occur at lower elevations (up to 2700 m) and I. tenuituba occurs at higher elevations (above 3100 m). The occurrence of these clines provides an excellent opportunity to gain insight into the nature of hybrid zones in plants. We plan to use frequencies based on RAPD markers and isozymes to characterize the shape of the cline between I. aggregata and I. tenuituba in each of these areas and compare these molecular markers with morphological variation along the clines. 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
In addition we can use this data in combination with analysis of molecular variation (AMOVA) to examine population differentiation.

As human populations expand and our impact on other species becomes more prominent it is critical to understand what limits the distribution and abundance of species. Previous studies have examined the roles of ecological features such as habitat area and  energy availability, and historical influences such as plate tectonics and glaciations, on distribution patterns. However, animal performance is also limited by physiological factors such as oxygen availability, air density and metabolic rate. I am engaged in an integrative study which examines the influence of physiological performance on distribution and abundance patterns in hummingbirds along altitudinal gradients. My laboratory work at the University of Texas described how oxygen concentration, air density, wind speed, body mass and wing area affects maximal performance in Ruby-throated Hummingbirds. Field work in the Peruvian Andes is elucidating the influence of morphology on altitudinal range distribution and maximal flight performance for 57 species of hummingbirds that vary ten-fold in wing length and body mass. 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
I now propose to study how altitude and flight performance affect ecological features of hummingbird foraging. I will compare two species, Broad-tailed Hummingbirds and Rufous Hummingbirds, that differ in power requirements for flight and competitive ability but are both distributed from 1000 to 3800 meters in the Southwest United States. Low- and mid-elevation sites are in southeast Arizona at the Southwest Research Station of the American Museum of Natural History. The high elevation site is the Rocky Mountain Biological Laboratory in Gothic, Colorado.

	“There is nothing more insidious as an agent of extinction than exotic species.” -- so stated George Rabb, of the World Conservation Union (Culotta, 1991); this declaration underscores the need for research focusing on invasive species. Invasive species, also referred to as exotic, alien, and colonizing species, are those organisms that appear in habitats where they have not lived historically (Williamson, 1996). Some have acted as vectors for disease, promoted extinction, and inhibited human economic activities (Vitousek et al., 1996). Invaders have manifested themselves as a global threat to biodiversity and ecological integrity (Vitousek et al., 1996).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
	That few introduced species actually become “weeds” is asserted by the “tens rule”, which proposes that of all introductions or “escaped” taxa, only ten percent become self-sustaining and established (Williamson, 1996). Further, of those species that become established, ten percent eventually become “insidious…agent[s] of extinction” -- pests. What determines whether this fraction of species is able to proliferate, when so many introduced taxa apparently remain innocuous, is a crucial question for ecologists. Williamson’s (1996) categorizations begin to clarify this matter by distinguishing between organisms that are merely extant in a habitat and those that could actively cause negative change. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
      Many reasons have been posited to explain the success of invaders. Release from natural enemies and competitors may liberate the invader from those factors which most strongly limit its abundance in its habitat of origin (Crawley, 1987; Hoffman and Kearns, 1997). Successful invaders apparently already possess life history traits that are compatible with the conditions of their new environments (Th&eacute;baud et al., 1995). Invaders may invest an unusually large amount of energy into reproduction, and reproduce over shorter periods of time, relative to other species in the community, resulting in tremendous propagule pressure (Th&eacute;baud et al., 1996; Williamson, 1996). An invader may have  physical or chemical attributes that provide protection from predators — e.g., exotic plants may have tough leaves or secondary compounds that deter generalist herbivores. Broad ecological amplitude as an advantageous attribute is debatable; while flexibility may enable an exotic taxon to establish itself in various habitats, it may also hinder its subsequent proliferation, because native taxa may be better locally adapted (Baker, 1965). As noted in the tens rule, there is an important functional distinction between establishment and spread in the assessment of an invader’s status. 
	&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Exotics that become successfully established may do so by being competitively superior to the native species with which they interact. Considering plants in particular, such interactions may be best conceptualized as forms of “interference” (Harper, 1977; p. 365). Interference describes differential growth caused by unequal resource accessibility, and includes both competitive and non-competitive aspects. Competitive interference is the differential use of a resource that is equally available to more than one plant species at the same time. The consequence of utilization of a finite resource by multiple species occupying similar niche types is, according to conventional wisdom, negative for all competing species. Competitive interference is a direct phenomenon. Non-competitive interference refers to a situation in which the dominant taxon indirectly affects the ability of another to grow. The dominant taxon has access to resources unavailable to the other plants, an example being the ability to absorb soil nutrients in a manner unique to that species alone. This asymmetric utilization allows it to surpass the growth capacities of other species in the habitat, without ever directly competing for the same resource. For a species to become a weed, may require that it possesses attributes making it a superior direct competitor. By contrast, the ability to capitalize on disturbance may present an opportunity for non-competitive interference. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
      Plants can compete for two sorts of resources (Tilman, 1987): those above ground and those below. Competition for light, the above-ground resource, is not likely for plants growing where canopy cover across all species is less than 100%. In contexts where canopies do interact, interception of radiation by a faster-growing plant often results in the enhanced fecundity of that plant relative to shaded neighbors (Tilman, 1987). Below ground, plants competitively exploit nutrients -- such as phosphorus -- leading to differential abilities to develop both below- and above-ground biomass (Caldwell et al., 1985). Better access to resources and more efficient translation of resources into biomass are competitive abilities that may favor invaders over natives with which they interact.

Flowers receive pollen grains and disseminate them to other plants.  New mathematical theory predicts how variation in pollen presentation should affect reproductive success:  paradoxically, when pollinating animals visit frequently, plants that release pollen from their anthers in smaller doses per visit will donate more pollen to other plants.  If so, current practices for evaluating pollinators are inadequate and misleading, but the theory is untested.  This work tests predictions about how floral features that control pollen dosing should change with changes in pollination rate and mode.  The study group shows great variation in anther function, as well as numerous apparent shifts in pollination mode.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
The morphology and function of flowers will be studied in many species to clarify how dosing is achieved and modulated.  A “family tree” of Penstemon will be derived using DNA sequences and other characters to estimate genetic similarities, so that observed changes in floral characters can be interpreted with a proper understanding of species relationships.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
As the first test of pollen presentation theory, this work will help decide whether these models are correct and useful.  The genetic tree will provide a firm basis for future work on other aspects of floral function, establishing this particularly diverse genus as a model system.  Improved knowledge of pollination mechanics would improve the economic evaluation of alternative pollinators of agricultural crops.

Variation in the environment can take several forms.  Organisms may be exposed to different weather or climate conditions over space and/or time.  The predators, competitors or food items in their community may also change over space and/or time.  I am exploring how such changes affect individual reproduction and survival, and through that, changes in population numbers over time, with the aim of generating general understanding that is broadly applicable to other organisms. My present research utilizes butterflies as study systems, and has four focii.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
First, I am looking at the effects of an introduced plant species on the population dynamics and evolution of host plant choice of a native butterfly species.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
Second, I am exploring the effects of variation in adult and larval food availability on reproduction and survival, thence population numbers.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
Third, I am examining the role of “puddling”, or feeding by butterflies on mud, dung or carrion, in the nutritional ecology of the butterflies.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
Finally, I am testing hypotheses concerning the role of climatic constraints in determining the limits of the species’ distributions, with implications for impacts of climate change on ranges.

Soil structure plays an important role in both thecarbon cycle and the hydrological cycle.  Across the landscape thedistribution of soil texture and chemistry, and therefore the distributionof plant species, is influenced by parent material.  And yet, over the long term, plants engineer their environment by incorporating carbon from the atmosphere into soil,modifying soil structure and improving its ability to hold water through dry periods.  Global climate modelssuggest that one effect of increased atmospheric carbon dioxide levels(from fossil fuel burning) is a decline in summer precipitation in themid-continental US, soit is worth investigating how different soils will buffer such a shortage.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
	The goal of this project is to understand how important the role of organicsoil carbon is relative to the role of soil grain size distribution indetermining soil&#039;s moisture holding capacity and how the vegetation and/ororganic matter on different soiltypes affects the rate at which water moves from the soil to theatmosphere.  Results from this study should help explain how differentsoils will react to the same climate perturbation and improve ecosystemscientists&#039; ability to predict the effect of a given change in precipitation on local vegetation patterns.

I am investigating the population genetics of yellow-bellied marmots (Marmota flaviventris) in the upper East River Valley.  These large rodents exhibit at least three characteristics that may influence how genes are distributed in a population.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
First, marmots commonly live in social groups (often composed of related individuals) within colonies. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
 Second, marmots are distributed in a patchy manner throughout the East River Valley (they are found only in certain rocky, open meadows that provide suitable burrow sites).  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
Third, almost all male marmots disperse (that is, leave their natal burrow area) in their first year, while one half of the females are philopatric (that is, they stay to breed in their natal burrow area).  Specifically, I am interested in how these behavioral and ecological characteristics influence the distribution of genetic variation in the marmot population.  My research involves live-trapping marmots, collecting tissue samples for genetic analysis, and releasing the marmots unharmed at the site of capture.

