):  Linaria vulgaris is an ideal organism for testing questions about nectar robbing, because of its attractiveness to bumblebees and its natural history.  Specifically, Linaria’s nectar spur is extensively robbed at RMBL, which has direct and easily measurable effects on pollinator behaviour.  In addition, its large inflorescences, long flowering season, tolerance to drought, and versatility (in terms of growth from cuttings and transplants) make it ideal for experimental work.  Linaria also has several convenient properties, such as transparent nectar spurs and easily collected fruit.  Finally, I believe that my project ideas involving this invasive species are worthwhile specifically because this system will allow me to investigate ecosystem-level effects of Linaria’s presence on local insect communities.  I am interested in testing whether this rather drought-resistant species is important in maintaining insect diversity when holes made by robbers allow access to nectar to non-pollinators; this effect is likely important in dry years when other richly rewarding flowers are scarce.  
I am aware that RMBL’s goal is to ultimately remove Linaria from the townsite, and I will therefore go to particular lengths to ensure that I am not increasing its chances of spreading either through rhizomes or seeds.  I wish to use only existing patches of Linaria, or potted plants that I will transplant from these existing patches, thereby precluding clonal propagation.  I will also be removing all fruits before dehiscence to measure seed set and this will effectively prevent seed dispersal as well.  To prevent gene flow through foreign pollen, I will use only clonal propagules collected from plants at RMBL.  

In addition, I will be working with various bumblebee species, and, to a lesser extent, with other insect species feeding on Linaria.

ResearchPlanAbstract:
&lt;p&gt;Biological invasions are second only to habitat destruction with respect to their impact on biodiversity, with severe consequences on native ecosystems and the economy well documented (e.g. Drake et. al., 1989). However, invasion ecology, whether taken from the point of view of the ecologist or the weed scientist, has in the past typically focused research on the negative impacts that an alien species has on a native species/community (e.g. competition, herbivory), or vice versa (e.g. herbivory, parasitism). Indeed research investigating positive interactions is comparatively depauperate (Kareiva &amp; Bertness, 1997), despite the acknowledgement that facilitative interactions between native and alien species is widespread and can accelerate the alien species&#039; invasion and establishment into native communities.
&lt;/p&gt;
 &lt;p&gt;    Even more understudied however, are the potential facilitative interactions among alien invaders themselves (Parker et. al., 1999; Grosholz, 2005). With alien species historically established, and an increasing number of new invaders spreading globally, positive interactions - whether direct or indirect - are inevitable. Such facilitation has been shown to turn an otherwise benign invader into an aggressively expanding one as a result of positive feedback between the invaders (Grosholz 2005). Frequent species introductions could therefore have a synergistic effect at a range of scales, and has been termed &#039;invasional meltdown&#039; by Simberloff &amp; Von Holle (1999). Evidence for this has been documented in coastal (Grosholz, 2005), fresh water (Ricciardi, 2001) and terrestrial systems (O&#039;Dowd, 2003), but research on facilitative interactions between alien species still remains scarce. 
&lt;/p&gt;
 &lt;p&gt;    The aim of this study is to investigate the impact of two alien species on each other&#039;s reproduction, this effect being mediated via shared pollinators.  Linaria vulgaris and Circium arvense ,are two established alien plant invader species found in the Rocky Mountains. Measures of reproductive success (fruit and seed set) and rates of insect visitation to these plant species will be compared between populations that have been manipulated to represent a range of relative frequencies where these invader species co-occur.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The location of treeline is related to climate conditions, such as the temperature of soil during the summer and whether there is enough water for trees to grow. With global warming, both temperatures and precipitation are likely to change in the Rocky Mountain region. These changes could reasonably lead to shifts in the location of treeline. This project studies the sensitivity of trees growing near treeline to climate, and the potential environmental consequences of a shift in treeline location.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Globally, both high elevation and low elevation treeline are related to climate, with high elevation treeline controlled by root zone temperature and low elevation treeline determined by water availability. Recent climate trends, and predictions for climate change in the Rocky Mountain region, show temperature and precipitation changes, which could reasonably result in shifts in treeline position. Forest differs from both steppe and alpine meadow vegetation in terms of climate-ecosystem interactions, including energy, water and carbon exchanges. As a result, shifts in treeline have the potential to alter regional (and even global) climate if they are widespread. I am proposing some preliminary monitoring and sampling to look for evidence that upper and lower treeline near RMBL are sensitive to climate change, and to estimate the differences among forest, alpine meadow, and sagebrush-steppe ecosystems in terms of energy, water and carbon cycling. This work will build on past research by John Harte, John&#039;s REU students, and myself, and will involve new collaboration with Chris Still. I also hope to integrate students from my 2006 course into the monitoring/sampling effort.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Natural flow regimes of rivers are undergoing considerable modification due to global change and human use of water.  This research will contribute to understanding how natural variation in flow influences the function of river ecosystems. The grazing of algae by insect larvae is a fundamentally important interaction in rivers that is affected by the flow regime. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In some streams large amounts of algae result from &#039;trophic cascades&#039; whereby predators like trout suppress grazing leading to increases in algae. In other streams insect grazers are able to keep algae in check. Extreme events like floods and droughts can influence grazer-algal interactions by affecting the abundance and type of grazers, numbers of trout, algal growth rates, and availability of nutrients.  The effects of variation in flow regimes on grazer-algal interactions will be measured across a river-landscape to unravel the controls on the strength of trophic cascades.&lt;/p&gt; 

&lt;p&gt;This work focuses on species interactions fundamental to many ecosystems; thus it will contribute to producing broadly applicable ecological generalities. &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Furthermore, the project builds on 30 years of research on streams in the East River valley near the Rocky Mountain Biological Laboratory in Western Colorado.  The fate of water resources is hotly debated in Western Colorado, like many other areas around the world.  By advancing understanding of the effects of natural variation in flow regimes on river inhabitants, the research will provide knowledge valuable for the management of rivers with human-induced changes to river flow.&lt;/p&gt;

In this study I will investigate the role of climate change in ecologic variation in plant leaf functional traits. Specifically, I will: 
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
Assess the importance of acclimation and/or adaptation by comparing population-level changes in functional traits. I will do this by-
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
(A)	Comparing leaf traits on and off an experimental treatment where plant communities have been subject to experimental warming. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
(B)	Comparing plant species populations at high and low elevations. Environmental gradients influence how plants respond to environmental change; we are looking to find the ways in which plants respond.  Is their response passive, or are they adapting?  The sorting of species diversity and abundance is determined by filters, either biotic or abiotic. It is thought that differential survival of plants i.e., surviving filters is due to changes in plant functional traits.

&lt;p&gt;Most organisms adjust their behaviors and physiological processes to the changing environmental conditions that occur between day and night.  In nature, animals exhibit patterns of activities that approximate a 24-hour period or circadian rhythm.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Animals must also respond to seasonal cycles as well as circadian cycles.  In recent studies, there is a great body of evidence to support the idea that these the rhythms of activity, both external and internal are controlled by hormones.  The yellow-bellied marmot (Marmota flaviventris) is a ground-dwelling squirrel that must gain significant mass over the summer to ensure over-winter survival.  Yellow-bellied marmots hibernate and do not feed during hibernation, but must rely on stored body fat during winter.  In this study, I propose to study the role of hormones known to control hunger and satiety in controlling the daily and seasonal fluctuations in feeding as a means of storing body fat for hibernation.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial;&quot;&gt;Woodpeckers are ecologically very important because
they excavate nest cavities that eventually become available to many other
species that require cavities but cannot create their own. In the aspen
woodlands of western
&lt;st1:place w:st=&quot;on&quot;&gt;North America&lt;/st1:place&gt;, red-naped
sapsuckers (Sphyrapicus nuchalis) are the dominant woodpecker, providing
essential nesting habitat for several other bird species. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial;&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial;&quot;&gt;Sapsuckers also
create sap wells in willows (Salix sp.), providing a nutritious resource for
&gt; 40 species, including hummingbirds, chipmunks, and bees. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial;&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial;&quot;&gt;Previous work by
Gretchen Daily and colleagues suggested that sapsuckers avoid nesting in aspen
groves located &gt; 800 m from willows, indicating that reproductive success is
limited by travel distance between nest cavity and willows. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial;&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial;&quot;&gt;This hypothesis was
supported by our study of sapsuckers in
&lt;st1:place w:st=&quot;on&quot;&gt;
&lt;st1:city w:st=&quot;on&quot;&gt;Gunnison
County&lt;/st1:city&gt;,
&lt;st1:state w:st=&quot;on&quot;&gt;Colorado&lt;/st1:state&gt;&lt;/st1:place&gt; in
June-August 2005, which found that nestlings in aspens located further from
willows were fed significantly less often than those closer to willows.
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial;&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial;&quot;&gt;However, in 2006, when we repeated our study we found no relationship. Our
conflicting results cast doubt on the importance of willows to sapsucker nest
site choice. Also, previous research did not directly measure sapsucker nest
densities and did not consider the potentially &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial;&quot;&gt;confounding effect of the fungus, Phellinus
tremuloides. Sapsuckers nest only in aspens infected with P. tremuloides
because this fungus softens the aspen heartwood. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial;&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial;&quot;&gt;Thus, in summer 2007, we
propose to test the strength of relationship between willow proximity and
sapsucker nest tree choice by directly measuring sapsucker density and willow
proximity at the landscape scale and including fungus distribution as a
potential causal factor.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;MsoNormal&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Woodrats. &lt;/u&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;For animals that are small and nocturnal, like
bushy-tailed woodrats, data on the mating system and dispersal patterns are
impossible to collect with direct observation.&lt;span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;At several sites in
&lt;st1:place w:st=&quot;on&quot;&gt;
&lt;st1:placename w:st=&quot;on&quot;&gt;Gunnison&lt;/st1:placename&gt;
&lt;st1:placetype w:st=&quot;on&quot;&gt;County&lt;/st1:placetype&gt;&lt;/st1:place&gt; I have monitored
resident and immigrant populations of woodrats since 1985. &lt;span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;Thus, I have a wealth of data on longevity,
reproduction, mortality and relatedness within populations.&lt;span&gt;  &lt;/span&gt;Modern DNA fingerprinting techniques in
conjunction with the field data, allow determination of paternity, relatedness,
and patterns of immigration and emigration by looking directly at DNA sequences
of individuals.&lt;span&gt;  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;MsoNormal&quot;&gt;
&lt;o:p&gt; &lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;MsoNormal&quot;&gt;
&lt;o:p&gt; &lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10pt; font-family: Arial;&quot;&gt;Cattle and Elk
Grazing.&lt;span&gt;  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10pt; font-family: Arial;&quot;&gt;In several habitats I have measured plant
productivity, diversity and invasive species on grazed and ungrazed land.&lt;span&gt;  &lt;/span&gt;These data have been collected several times
each summer since 1992.&lt;span&gt;  &lt;/span&gt;Two noteworthy
results from this continuing long-term monitoring are that in one habitat,
there were no significant differences between grazed and ungrazed plant
communities, and in a different habitat, elimination of grazing reduced plant
productivity.&lt;span&gt;  &lt;/span&gt;At lower elevations,
transitional zones are used by elk in the fall and winter.&lt;span&gt;  &lt;/span&gt;With continuing exurban development and
increase in the size of elk herds, this sagebrush habitat may be is dwindling
in size and may be experiencing overuse.&lt;span&gt;  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

Monitoring plant cover in large exclosures allows assessment of use.  Potential loss of cover with over-utilization might also have negative implications for the Gunnison sage grouse populations.

