ResearchPlanAbstract:
&lt;p class=\\\&quot;MsoNormal\\\&quot;&gt;Plants form the base in terrestrial food webs, whereas algae
form the ‘green’ base of aquatic food webs. In rocky streams, algae are usually
dominated by diatoms, which form brownish slippery coats on stones. Diatoms are
small, one-celled algae that have beautiful glass-like outer walls and store
energy as oil – making them highly nutritious for grazing insects and snails.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=\\\&quot;MsoNormal\\\&quot;&gt;
&lt;o:p&gt; &lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=\\\&quot;MsoNormal\\\&quot;&gt;My research is on the interaction of diatoms and their stone
substrates. Previous experiments have shown that stone chemistry has little
effect on diatoms. In contrast, the small-scale surface texture of stones
influences diatoms by providing crevices that protect diatoms from grazers and
floods.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=\\\&quot;MsoNormal\\\&quot;&gt;
&lt;o:p&gt; &lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=\\\&quot;MsoNormal\\\&quot;&gt;This summer I will extend this research to begin
investigating whether the diatoms protected by stone texture affect stream food
webs. Diatoms multiply quickly, such that diatoms protected in crevices during
intense grazing multiply may ‘spill out’, providing additional food for grazers
(and allow more grazer growth). Similarly, diatoms in crevices survive
rain-associated floods and these diatoms may colonize areas that have lost
diatoms because of stone rolling during floods and thereby help algae rebound
(= contribute food to the food web).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=\\\&quot;MsoNormal\\\&quot;&gt;
&lt;o:p&gt; &lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=\\\&quot;MsoNormal\\\&quot;&gt;These investigations will use surveys to measure algae and
invertebrate densities on different types and textures of stones, and stream
experiments to look at the recovery of algae on stones subjected to flood-like
conditions and determine whether invertebrates track this recovery. If time and
conditions permit, I hope to also test whether rough stones ‘grow’ mayflies
better than smooth stones.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=\\\&quot;MsoNormal\\\&quot;&gt;
&lt;o:p&gt; &lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
